# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the mpo_vulkan_* tests only (F25,
# profiles/mpo/tests/CMakeLists.txt sets LSAN_OPTIONS on them, with ASan).
# Nothing else uses this file.
#
# Why: the Linux gcc asan-ubsan CI job failed now and then with small indirect
# leaks (224 to 560 bytes, 56-byte blocks) allocated on threads that lavapipe
# starts. Every frame between calloc and asan_thread_start is in the driver,
# which the Vulkan loader had already unloaded at vkDestroyInstance, so LSan
# prints "<unknown module>" or a stale name of another library mapped later at
# those addresses (libasan.so, libubsan.so, the test itself). The blocks are
# only pointed to from the unloaded driver's data. With the same Mesa
# (25.2.8-0ubuntu0.24.04.4) it reproduces here too.
#
# The fix is VK_LOADER_DISABLE_DYNAMIC_LIBRARY_UNLOADING=1 (the Vulkan
# loader of Ubuntu 24.04, 1.3.275, has it; an older one ignores it), set on
# the same tests: the driver stays loaded, its data
# still points to the blocks and LSan reports nothing. These lines are a
# fallback, for frames in the driver or the layers.
#
# What stays covered: a leak in our code has its frames in the test binary or
# our libraries, which are never unloaded, so no line below matches it. A
# Vulkan object we forget to destroy is reported by the validation layer
# (MPO_VK_REQUIRE_VALIDATION=1 in CI), not by LSan.
#
# LSan suppresses a leak when any frame of its allocation stack matches a
# line, by module, function or file name (substring).

# Frames in a library that was unloaded anyway.
leak:<unknown module>
# lavapipe (and the LLVM it links), the Vulkan loader and the validation layer.
leak:libvulkan_lvp.so
leak:libLLVM.so
leak:libvulkan.so
leak:libVkLayer_khronos_validation.so
